NAME
       hostname - show or set the system's host name

SYNOPSIS
       hostname [-s|-f|-d|-i|-I]
       hostname NAME

DESCRIPTION
       With no argument hostname prints the current host name. With a NAME,
       root can change the name the running system uses, but only until the
       next boot: hostname does not write /etc/hostname. To change the name
       for good on RHEL 9 use hostnamectl set-hostname.

OPTIONS
       -s, --short
              Print the name up to the first dot (server1).

       -f, --fqdn, --long
              Print the full name (server1.lab.example.com).

       -d, --domain
              Print the DNS domain: the part after the first dot.

       -i, --ip-address
              Print the address the host name resolves to.

       -I, --all-ip-addresses
              Print every address of the machine's interfaces (not loopback).

EXAMPLES
              $ hostname
              server1.lab.example.com
              $ hostname -s
              server1
              $ hostname -I
              192.168.50.11

FILES
       /etc/hostname

SEE ALSO
       hostnamectl(1), hosts(5)
